From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: knapcio <knapcio@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 02:30:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 26/30] decode attention: G6_ATTN_POSIDX=1 lists the keys by
 position at buckets up to 2048 (E1, default off)

At a capacity <= 2048 the top-2048 selection keeps every key with a finite indexer score, i.e. every key at a
position <= the row's own, so the indexer only decides the slots' order (the fp32 order of normalize and the
context sums on every layer). With G6_ATTN_POSIDX=1 a full-index call of capacity <= 2048 writes the
positional index instead (pos_index: slot j = key j up to the row's position, -1 after) and skips prep_index,
index_scores and the selector; under G6_ATTN_FRONT the ramp takes prep_index's blocks of attn_front. The
step then skips that layer's idx_q GEMV (posidx_step, decided from the replay entry the call runs, so it is
skipped exactly where the call does not read it); under G6_IDXQ_SPLIT its gather still runs, so the gather
fabric's collectives keep their order on every rank. append_index, idx_k and idx_w stay (the index cache is
needed past 2048).

Larger buckets (2049 keys and up), the shared layers' reuse, the CP entry points and the v2 prefill are
unchanged. The set of keys and every key's score are those of the indexer path; normalize and context sum
them in position order (E1, deterministic). A nonfinite indexer score no longer drops its key (error 4). The
flag is read once, is in the S2 runtime signature, and is off by default: with it off nothing changes.
